Tension headache
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Bilateral band-like pain, Dull non-pulsating character, Lack of nausea/vomiting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-047
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, continuous severe head pain lasting four days, causing extreme distress and inability to work.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a severe, continuous headache persisting for 96 hours that is entirely unresponsive to over-the-counter analgesia, causing severe emotional distress and sleep deprivation. Urgent assessment is required to exclude dangerous secondary causes of prolonged headache (e.g., meningitis, temporal arteritis) and provide immediate, potent pharmacological relief before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head has been locked in a vice for four days straight. The pressure is driving me crazy and nothing I take helps."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the gradual onset of a distinct mild to moderate, explicitly dull, completely non-pulsating head pain. The pain is bilateral, distinctly generalized, and strictly described as a tight, severely constricting band completely encircling the entire head. The pain is explicitly not aggravated by routine, basic physical activity such as walking or climbing stairs. There is a complete, absolute absence of severe nausea or forceful vomiting during this attack. The patient notes a distinctly prolonged duration of severe pain, having lasted continuously for four days. The pain was explicitly triggered by severe emotional stress at work and severe distinct sleep deprivation over the past week. There is a strict, absolute lack of a severe, fully reversible, clearly stereotyped preceding distinct neurological aura.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently unable to concentrate or perform occupational duties due to continuous pain and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe stress and anxiety regarding the unyielding nature of the pain; poor sleep hygiene.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 78 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, visibly stressed, rubbing the back of the neck and temples continuously.
Physical Exam: Physical examination reveals frequent, marked severe tenderness definitively palpated entirely throughout the distinct pericranial, cervical, and strictly occipital musculature. Neurological examination is completely unremarkable; cranial nerves II-XII are intact, no focal motor or sensory deficits, and deep tendon reflexes are symmetrical. Crucially, there is absolutely no nuchal rigidity (neck is supple), and Kernig's/Brudzinski's signs are negative. Fundoscopic exam reveals sharp optic disc margins (no papilledema). Temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed: bilateral band-like pain, dull non-pulsating character, and complete lack of nausea/vomiting.
Environmental Observations: Desk covered in paperwork; laptop open in the bedroom; bright overhead lighting.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1000mg and Ibuprofen 400mg taken repeatedly with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension-Type Headache - Status: Acute Prolonged Exacerbation
Differentials:

Migraine without Aura - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is bilateral and strictly non-pulsating (band-like), it is not aggravated by physical activity, and there is a complete absence of nausea, severe photophobia, or phonophobia.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, completely normal neck flexion (no meningismus), and absence of altered mental status.

Temporal Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient is under 50 years old, temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile, and jaw claudication/visual changes are entirely absent.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from a severely prolonged, intractable tension-type headache driven by profound muscular spasm and psychosocial stress. The unyielding nature of the pain for four consecutive days triggered immense anxiety regarding a serious intracranial pathology, prompting the after-hours call. Delaying assessment would subject the patient to further agonizing discomfort, escalating health anxiety, and continued severe sleep deprivation. Urgent clinical examination successfully and definitively ruled out life-threatening secondary causes of headache (like meningitis or increased intracranial pressure), allowing for the immediate initiation of targeted muscle relaxants and potent NSAIDs to break the chronic pain cycle, strictly fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent, necessary intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Diazepam 5mg orally STAT and at night for 2 days strictly for pericranial muscle relaxation and sleep induction. Prescribed Naproxen 500mg BD with food for robust anti-inflammatory relief.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly applying a warm, moist heat pack to the back of the neck and shoulders for 20 minutes every evening, the patient actively forces the pericranial musculature to vasodilate and relax, immediately reducing the mechanical tension pulling on the skull.
Step 2: By enforcing a rigid "screen curfew" two hours before bed, the patient actively prevents blue-light stimulation of the brain, directly facilitating the onset of deep, restorative sleep to break the stress-headache cycle.
Step 3: By utilizing an ergonomic chair and ensuring the computer monitor is exactly at eye level, the patient actively prevents sustained, poor cervical posture, completely removing the primary mechanical trigger for future tension attacks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to discuss stress management and ergonomic adjustments.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if the headache suddenly changes to the "worst pain of your life" within seconds, if you develop a high fever and stiff neck, or if you develop new weakness or numbness on one side of your body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Tension headaches are highly responsive to lifestyle modifications and stress reduction; medications are only a short-term bridge.
Clinical Course: The muscle relaxant and strong anti-inflammatory will break the severe pain cycle within 24 hours. A mild, residual dull ache may persist for another day as the neck muscles fully recover.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the pain completely fails to improve after 48 hours on the new medications, or if you develop severe nausea and vomiting.

